-- 1. Aggregation & Distinct Counts (HAVING Clause)

-- Occasionally, athletes change nationalities and compete for different countries. Write a query to identify athletes who have represented more than one distinct National Olympic Committee (NOC) throughout their careers. Join the athletes, results, and nocs tables. Return the athlete's full_name and the count of distinct country_names they have represented.

select 
    full_name
    ,count(distinct country_name)
from til_data_engineering.olympics.athletes as ath
    join  til_data_engineering.olympics.results as res
        on ath.athlete_id = res.athlete_id
    join  til_data_engineering.olympics.nocs as nocs
        on res.noc_id = nocs.noc
group by full_name
having count(distinct country_name) > 1 
;
-- 2. Date Math & Aggregation

-- The committee wants to know the average age of medal winners at the 2008 Olympics. Write a query joining athletes, results, and editions. Filter for the edition_year 2008 and only include rows where a medal was awarded. Calculate the athlete's age at the competition_start_date, and then find the average age of these medalists. Return a single number, rounded to one decimal place.

select 
round(avg(datediff(year,date_of_birth,competition_start_date))) as avg_age 
from til_data_engineering.olympics.athletes as ath
    join  til_data_engineering.olympics.results as res
        on ath.athlete_id = res.athlete_id
    join til_data_engineering.olympics.editions as ed 
        on res.edition_id = ed.edition_id
where ed.edition_year = 2008 and medal is not null 
limit 1


;
-- 3. Grouping by Multiple Columns & HAVING

-- We want to find athletes who competed in both Team and Solo events during the exact same Olympic edition. Write a query using the results and athletes tables. Group your data by the athlete's full_name and the edition_id. Use a HAVING clause to filter for athletes who have exactly 2 distinct team_or_solo values within that specific edition. Return the full_name and the edition_id.

select 
full_name
,count(distinct team_or_solo)
,edition_id
from til_data_engineering.olympics.results as res
    left join til_data_engineering.olympics.athletes as ath
        on res.athlete_id = ath.athlete_id
group by full_name, edition_id
having count(distinct team_or_solo) > 1




;
-- 4. Math & Basic Window Functions (RANK)

-- Which host cities had the highest participant-to-medal ratios? Write a query using the editions table to calculate the ratio of total_participants divided by total_medals for each edition. Use the RANK() window function to rank the editions from the highest ratio to the lowest. Return the edition_year, host_city, the calculated ratio (rounded to two decimal places), and the rank.


with cte as (
    select
        host_city
        ,total_participants/total_medals as medal_ratio
    from til_data_engineering.olympics.editions
)


select 
    host_city
    ,medal_ratio
    ,rank() over (partition by host_city order by medal_ratio desc) as rank_
from cte 
qualify rank_ = 1


;
